Cray Comparative Debugger Source Repository INSTALL
CCDB: Cray Comparative Debugger
Copyright (c) 1996-2011 by Guardsoft Pty Ltd.
©2007-2011 Cray Inc.  All Rights Reserved

SEE LICENSE FOR ADDITIONAL COPYRIGHT AND USAGE INFORMATION.

1. CRAY SUPPORTED PLATFORMS

 CCDB has been confirmed to run on both SLES10 and SLES11 SuSE distributions.
 It is supported on both seastar and gemini interconnects.

2. CRAY SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS

 The Cray supported tools/libraries that ccdb uses and needs for proper installation are:
  - Cray Tool Interface (INCLUDED IN BUILD - see extras)
    Note that the .SLES10 version is intended to be used for SLES 10 builds.
  - Editline libraries (INCLUDED IN BUILD - see extras)
  - libaif libraries (Originally from Monash/GUARDSoft - INCLUDED IN BUILD - see libaif)
  - libmi libraries (Originally from Eclipse PTP - INCLUDED IN BUILD - see libmi)

 The third party tools/libraries that ccdb uses and needs for proper installation are:
  - GNU make
  - GNU compiler
  - gdb (includes ddt patches provided by Allinea - INCLUDED IN BUILD - see extras)
  - MRNet (Provided by UW-Madison/Paradyn - INCLUDED IN BUILD - see extras)

 In addition, to build the demo sources the following third party tools are needed:
  - MPI libraries

3. Cray Recommended Build Process

 CCDB uses the GNU autotools suite to discover the platform specific configuration 
 parameters and generate makefiles. CCDB is a joint project with Monash University 
 and GUARDSoft Ltd. and is supported on a wide variety of machines and interfaces. 
 In order to address differences between these different platforms, a homogenous 
 tool such as autoconf makes the build process much smoother from platform to platform.

 Since the configure process can be rather lengthy and complex, a helper script named
 ccdb_build_tool.sh has been included in the top level directory to assist the user
 with the build process. This script provides a human readable interface in order
 to abstract away the complexities associated with the autotools suite.

 Before running the script, it is required that no program environment (i.e. PrgEnv-*)
 module is loaded to ensure that all third party tools are properly configured for the
 users environment. The user should check to ensure that no PrgEnv modules are loaded
 (determined by running the `module list` command and observing the output). If a 
 PrgEnv module is found to be loaded, the user should unload the module with the 
 following command:

  $ module unload PrgEnv-xxx

 Where PrgEnv-xxx is the loaded PrgEnv module. Once it is determined that no PrgEnv
 module is loaded the user environment is ready to build the source files.
 
 The script uses two directories: ccdb_install and ccdb_tmp. The ccdb_install directory
 is where the ccdb install files are delivered to. The ccdb_tmp directory is used as
 a temporary staging directory for the install of third party tools (such as gdb).
 The script extracts the required files from this directory and is removed during
 cleanup.

 When using this script, the user is prompted for build options. The user should enter
 either "y" (for yes) or "n" (for no) to the presented options. The following is a list
 explaining each option and how it is used. Note that all listed dependencies  must 
 either be built or have previously been built and their compiled components exist in 
 the ccdb_tmp directory.

 BUILD OPTIONS

 1. libaif

    This library provides an architecture neutral way to talk about data. AIF stands for
    Architecture Independent Format. It is used for much of ccdb's communication of
    data and facilitates comparisons between different platforms.

 2. libmi

    This library provides a machine interface that ccdb's debug server uses in order to
    facilitate communication with gdb. Originally this was written for Eclipse PTP,
    however it does contain Cray specific modifications and improvements.

 3. MRNet

    This tool provides the communication network for ccdb. It allows ccdb to use a scalable
    overlay network to communicate between the frontend client and the backend debug
    servers. It is developed entirely third party with occasional Cray provided support by
    the University of Wisconsin - Madison through the Paradyn Tools Project.

 4. gdb

    This is the gnu project debugger. It provides all the debugging facilities of ccdb.
    If the user enters "n" for this option, the script will search the users PATH for "gdb"
    and automatically use any gdb that was found in PATH. It is highly recommended that the
    user uses the included gdb distribution as it contains custom patches created by Allinea
    for their ddt debugger. If the included gdb distribution was previously built (by entering
    "y"), the gdb binary located in the ccdb_tmp directory will be used instead of a gdb found
    by searching the users PATH.

 5. editline

    This library provides a BSD style alternative to the GNU readline library. It contains 
    numerous Cray provided improvements and is different than the original distribution. It
    is used to provide advanced functionality (such as command history and tab completion)
    for ccdb's command line interface.

 6. ccdb

    This is the main component of the build script. It builds the ccdb sources and produces
    the ccdb binaries.

 7. cleanup

    This option will automatically run `make clean` and `make distclean` inside of each
    of the selected components build tree (i.e. by entering "y"). Note that this option
    will not remove the ccdb_tmp directory.

 8. Advanced mode

    This option is meant only for developers who wish to enable added functionality that
    is not useful in a production environment. The following explains each of the 
    advanced mode options:

    A. Debug mode

       This enables printing of debug messages that give an internal view of program
       operation. This also allows the user to set the DBG_LOG_DIR environment variable
       to a cross mounted location to enable backend debug server logging.

    B. Optimization level

       This allows the user to specify a optimization level used to compile all selected
       tools/libraries. Note that by default this is set to -O2. This is useful for 
       profiling experiments and the use of other tools that require a specific optimization
       level.

    C. Perf log mode

       This enables performance statistics to be logged. The location that the log is 
       written to is set by the DBG_LOG_DIR environment variable.

    D. Backend Valgrind Mode

       This enables wrapping the backend debug server with valgrind. The valgrind logs
       will be written to the directory specified by the cross mounted location of
       DBG_LOG_DIR.

 The following is an example run of using the build tool.

 The script is invoked directly from the shell:

  $ ./ccdb_build_tool.sh

 A message display is shown followed by the first build option:

  ccdb build tool v1.2 - By andrewg@cray.com
  Please choose from the following options.

  A. Build libaif?[y/n]:

 The user then specifies their desired options to the script. In my example,
 I decided to build all pre-requisits and cleanup the build:

  A. Build libaif?[y/n]: y
  B. Build libmi?[y/n]: y
  C. Build MRNet?[y/n]: y
  D. Build gdb?[y/n]: y
  E. Build editline?[y/n]: y
  F. Build ccdb?[y/n]: y
  G. Cleanup build?[y/n]: y
  H. Advanced mode?[y/n]: n

 The build process then begins by displaying the paths it is using to the required include
 and library dependencies as well as a starting message:

  ################# Done parsing input ###################
  
  Using the following build/inst directories:
          Install directory: /cray/css/u6/andrewg/repositories/cray_svn/debugger-support/ccdb/ccdb_install
          Staging directory: /cray/css/u6/andrewg/repositories/cray_svn/debugger-support/ccdb/ccdb_tmp    
          Extras directory: /cray/css/u6/andrewg/repositories/cray_svn/debugger-support/ccdb/extras       
  
  Using the following paths to dependencies:
          path_alps_inc: /cray/css/u6/andrewg/repositories/cray_svn/debugger-support/ccdb/alps/include
          path_alps_lib: /cray/css/u6/andrewg/repositories/cray_svn/debugger-support/ccdb/alps/lib/alps
  
  ########################################################
  Starting build process...                               
  ########################################################
  
 The script then begins building the required third party tools and ccdb source files. This can
 be a lengthy process and the user should wait until the entire build is complete before using
 the shell again.

 Once completed, all ccdb install files are found inside of the ccdb_install directory.

4. Cray Build Configuration

 For users who do not wish to use the build script, or developers who are working on the tool,
 the first step in the build process is to run the configure script inside each of the source
 directories.

 This script is used to discover platform specific configuration parameters and was generated using
 the autoconf tool inside of the autotools suite.

 To invoke the script, the user should first `cd` into the desired directory and then call the configure
 script directly. In order to properly configure, the user should run the following command and review the
 possible options:

  $ ./configure --help

 This will output all possible configure options and will assit the user in making the proper decisions.

 The recommended options for each tool is the following:
  
  $STAGING_DIR is the location to deliver third party installs to.
  $INSTALL_DIR is the location to deliver the final ccdb install to.
  $ALPS_LIB_PATH is the location of the alps libaries.
  $ALPS_INC_PATH is the location of the alps includes.
  $CRAYTOOL_LIB_PATH is the location of the Cray Tool Interface libraries
  $CRAYTOOL_INC_PATH is the location of the Cray Tool Interface includes.

 Note that for the alps library and include paths it is recommended to provide the included files
 inside of the ./alps directory of the ccdb trunk instead of the alps libraries installed with CLE.
  
  libaif:
   --prefix=$STAGING_DIR
   
  libmi:
   --prefix=$STAGING_DIR

  MRNet:
   --prefix=$STAGING_DIR
   --with-startup=cray_xt
   --with-alpstoolhelp-inc=$ALPS_INC_PATH
   --with-alpstoolhelp-lib=$ALPS_LIB_PATH

  gdb:
   --prefix=$STAGING_DIR
   --disable-rpath
   --without-separate-debug-dir
   --without-gdb-datadir
   --with-mmalloc
   --without-python

  editline:
   --prefix=$STAGING_DIR

  ccdb:
   --prefix=$INSTALL_DIR
   --with-aif=$STAGING_DIR
   --with-rd
   --with-mrnet=$STAGING_DIR
   --with-cray
   --with-alps-lib=$ALPS_LIB_PATH
   --with-alps-inc=$ALPS_INC_PATH
   --with-craytool-lib=$CRAYTOOL_LIB_PATH
   --with-craytool-inc=$CRAYTOOL_INC_PATH
   --with-editline=$STAGING_DIR
   --with-gdbmi

 After running each configure script with the appropriate settings, Makefiles will be generated for
 all of the sources.

 SPECIAL NOTE:
 For developers who wish to enable debug mode for ccdb, in addtion to the above ccdb options
 the following should also be added to the configure arguments:

  --with-debug
  CFLAGS="-g -O0"

 Any flags can be manually modified by using the "FLAGS" argument to configure.

5. Compilation and Installation

 The following is the instructions to compile and properly install the source files.

 The order in which the tools should be built are:
  1. libaif
  2. libmi
  3. MRNet
  4. gdb
  5. editline
  6. ccdb

 To build libaif:
  libaif $ make
 
 After compilation is complete, run:
  libaif $ make install
  
 To build libmi:
  libmi $ make

 After compilation is complete, run:
  libmi $ make install

 To build MRNet:
  MRNet $ make

 After compilation is complete, run:
  MRNet $ make install

 At this point, the following steps should be conducted:

  1. The boost include files inside of the MRNet source tree's include directory
     should be delivered to the staging include directory.

  2. The Types.h file in the extras directory should be delivered to the staging
     include directory inside of the mrnet subdir.

  3. The mrnet commnode binary in the staging directory should be delivered to the
     final install directory (./bin).

  4. The mrnet_commnode_wrapper in the extras directory should be delivered to the
     final install directory (./bin).

 To build gdb:
  gdb $ make

 After compilation is complete, run:
  gdb $ make install

 At this point, the gdb binary inside of the $STAGING_DIR/bin directory should be delivered
 to $INSTALL_DIR/bin and renamed to gdb-ccdb.

 To build editline:
  editline $ make

 After compilation is complete, run:
  editline $ make install

 At this point, the editline dso's inside of the staging directory need to be copied to the
 final install directory.

 Before building ccdb, the included Cray Tool Interface distribution needs to be extracted to
 the staging directory so that the required dso's can be found.

 To build ccdb:
  guard $ make

 After compilation is complete, run:
  guard $ make install

 At this point, the following steps should be conducted:

  1. Deliver the dlaunch binary from the staging dir to the install dir.

  2. Deliver libaudit.so from the staging dir to the install dir.

  3. Deliver the alps libs from the staging dir to the install dir

  4. Deliver the libcraytool_backend and libcraytool_frontend dso's from
     the staging dir to the install dir.

  5. Modify and deliver the modulefile primer to the modulefiles directory
     inside of the final install dir.

 In order to cleanup the builds, in any of the directories run:
  $ make clean
  $ make distclean

6. Running and Testing

 There is currently a testsuite inside of the ccdb source tree, but it
 has not been integrated into the build process as of this writting.

 There are several demonstration programs included inside of the ccdb
 install directory within demos. The source files must be manually
 built using an application compiler such as PGI or Cray.

 It is recommended that the user switch to the PrgEnv-pgi or PrgEnv-cray
 modules before attempting to run make on the demo Makefiles.

 In order to use ccdb, the user must load the ccdb module or know how
 to properly modify their environment for ccdb. The user must have 
 compute node resources allocated using any of the Cray queueing systems.

 As a very basic example, lets say we have built the mpi_example and wish
 to run a simple test to ensure that ccdb is working properly. Lets also
 say that the current queueing system in use on the machine is PBS.

 The user would first enter an interactive qsub session:
 
  $ qsub -I -l mppwidth=8

 Then the user would load the ccdb module, or properly set their LD_LIBRARY_PATH variable:

  $ module load ccdb

 cd to the mpi_example directory and issue the ccdb command:

  mpi_example $ ccdb

 This starts a new ccdb session:

  ccdb - Cray Comparative Debugger
  Copyright (c) 2007-2011 Cray Inc. All Rights Reserved.
  Copyright (c) 1996-2011 by Guardsoft Pty Ltd
  
  dbg all>

 Issue the invoke command:

  dbg all> invoke $test ./hello_mpi
  Starting alps application, please wait...
  Creating MRNet communication network...
  Waiting for debug servers to attach to MRNet communications network...
  Timeout in 60 seconds. Please wait for the attach to complete.
  Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [0]
  Finalizing MRNet network setup...
  Waiting for dbgsrv callbacks...
  dbgsrv callback complete.
  Releasing application from the startup barrier...
  Invoke complete.
  [0]Initial breakpoint, __read_nocancel at :0
  dbg all>

 This verifies that the build was successful.

 For more advanced usage examples, refer to the docs directory.

7. ccdb Debuging

 If ccdb is built with debug mode turned on, the client will produce complex output that
 is only understandable by the developer. It is recommended that most users do not use
 the debug mode, but it can give insight into internal program operation. While using debug
 mode, before starting ccdb the user should export the variable DBG_LOG_DIR to their
 shell environment using a directory accessable by the compute nodes (such as a lustre
 filesystem).
 
 For example:
  export DBG_LOG_DIR=/path/to/logdir

 This will cause the debug servers running on the compute nodes to write their debug output
 to log files located in the specified directory.

